	{
		"level": 4, "title": "Arrays", "concept": "Many values in one variable",
		"pages": [
			{"title": "What is an array?",
			"text": "An [b]array[/b] stores [b]many values of the same type[/b] in one variable, like a shelf with numbered slots.\n\nInstead of three variables for a Mocha's ingredients, keep them in one array and handle them all with one loop.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int mocha[] = {COFFEE, CHOCOLATE, MILK};\n    printf(\"First ingredient: %s\\n\", mocha[0]);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays.php"},
			{"title": "Creating an array",
			"text": "• With values: [code]int prices[] = {30, 40, 45};[/code] (C counts the values: size 3)\n• With a size: [code]int counts[5];[/code] (5 slots, all 0 in the kitchen)\n• Size and some values: [code]int a[4] = {7};[/code] (7, 0, 0, 0)\n\nAll items have the [b]same type[/b], and the size can't change after the array is created.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int prices[] = {30, 40, 45};\n    int counts[5];\n    int a[4] = {7};\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", prices[2], counts[4], a[0]);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays.php"},
			{"title": "Indexes start at 0",
			"text": "Each item has an [b]index[/b] (its slot number) in [b][ ][/b]. The first item is [b][0][/b], not [1]!\n\nFor [code]int prices[] = {30, 40, 45};[/code]\n• prices[0] is 30\n• prices[1] is 40\n• prices[2] is 45 (the last index is size - 1)\n\nChange an item by assigning to its index: [code]prices[1] = 42;[/code]",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int prices[] = {30, 40, 45};\n    prices[1] = 42;\n    prices[2] += 5;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", prices[0], prices[1], prices[2]);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays.php"},
			{"title": "Looping through an array",
			"text": "A for loop and an array are best friends: the loop variable [b]i[/b] becomes the index.\n\n[code]for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)[/code] visits items 0, 1 and 2.\n\nHere one loop fetches every ingredient of a recipe, however many there are.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int egsilog[] = {RICE, EGG};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n        go_to(FRIDGE);\n        pick_up(egsilog[i]);\n        go_to(GRILL);\n        cook();\n        go_to(ASSEMBLY);\n        plate();\n    }\n    assemble(EGSILOG);\n    serve();\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_loop.php"},
			{"title": "How long is an array? sizeof",
			"text": "[b]sizeof(x)[/b] gives the size of x in [b]bytes[/b]: an int takes 4 bytes, so an array of 5 ints is 20 bytes.\n\nTo get the [b]number of items[/b], divide by the size of one item:\n\n[code]int n = sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]);[/code]\n\nThis keeps working when you add items to the list, so your loop never goes too far.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int prices[] = {30, 40, 45, 55, 60};\n    int n = sizeof(prices) / sizeof(prices[0]);\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        total += prices[i];\n    }\n    printf(\"%d dishes, total %d coins\\n\", n, total);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_size.php"},
			{"title": "Index out of range",
			"text": "Asking for a slot that doesn't exist is the most common array mistake. With 3 items, the indexes are 0, 1 and 2: there is no [3].\n\nIt usually comes from writing [b]<=[/b] instead of [b]<[/b] in the loop. In real C this silently reads garbage; in the kitchen you get a clear message instead:\n\n[i]m[3] doesn't exist. m has 3 items, numbered 0 to 2.[/i]",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int m[] = {COFFEE, CHOCOLATE, MILK};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {   // < 3, not <= 3\n        printf(\"%s\\n\", m[i]);\n    }\n}", "w3": "c_arrays.php"},
			{"title": "New kitchen functions: recipe() and cook_station()",
			"text": "Two Level 4 helpers made for arrays:\n• [b]recipe(DISH, items)[/b] fills the array [b]items[/b] with the dish's ingredients (one entry per piece) and [b]returns how many[/b]. Make the array big enough: [code]int items[8];[/code]\n• [b]cook_station(INGREDIENT)[/b] returns the station that cooks it, or [b]NOTHING[/b] if it's served fresh.\n\nWith them, one loop can make [b]any[/b] dish!",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int order = take_order();\n    int items[8];\n    int n = recipe(order, items);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        go_to(FRIDGE);\n        pick_up(items[i]);\n        int where = cook_station(items[i]);\n        if (where != NOTHING) {\n            go_to(where);\n            cook();\n        }\n        go_to(ASSEMBLY);\n        plate();\n    }\n    assemble(order);\n    serve();\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_loop.php"},
			{"title": "Reading the ticket queue: orders()",
			"text": "The tickets on the counter are a list, so arrays fit perfectly:\n• [b]orders(list)[/b] copies the dishes on the counter's tickets into your array, [b]oldest first[/b], and returns how many it copied. The tickets stay on the counter: it only looks.\n\nUse it to plan, for example to see how many burgers are waiting before you start the grill.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int list[10];\n    int n = orders(list);\n    int burgers = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        printf(\"Ticket %d: %s\\n\", i + 1, list[i]);\n        if (list[i] == HAMBURGER) {\n            burgers++;\n        }\n    }\n    printf(\"%d burgers waiting\\n\", burgers);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_loop.php"},
			{"title": "2D arrays: rows and columns",
			"text": "A [b]2D array[/b] is a table of [b]rows[/b] and [b]columns[/b] (a matrix).\n\n[code]int menu[2][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} };[/code]\n• The first [ ] is the number of rows (2), the second the number of columns (3).\n• Each row gets its own { }.\n• [code]menu[1][2][/code] means row 1, column 2: the value 6.\n• Change one cell with [code]menu[0][0] = 9;[/code]",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int sales[2][3] = {\n        {4, 2, 1},    // row 0: Monday    (Egsilog, Hotsilog, Tocilog)\n        {3, 5, 0}     // row 1: Tuesday\n    };\n    sales[1][2] = 2;  // Tuesday: 2 Tocilogs after all\n    printf(\"Monday Hotsilogs: %d\\n\", sales[0][1]);\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_multi.php"},
			{"title": "Looping through a 2D array",
			"text": "You need [b]one loop per dimension[/b]: an outer loop for the rows and an inner loop for the columns (the nested loops from Lesson 3).\n\nHere the Café menu is a table: each row is a drink, each column one of its ingredients, with NOTHING marking an empty slot.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int drinks[3] = {MACCHIATO, MOCHA, COLD_CHOCOLATE};\n    int recipes[3][3] = {\n        {COFFEE,    MILK,      NOTHING},\n        {COFFEE,    CHOCOLATE, MILK},\n        {CHOCOLATE, MILK,      ICE}\n    };\n    for (int row = 0; row < 3; row++) {\n        printf(\"%s:\", drinks[row]);\n        for (int col = 0; col < 3; col++) {\n            if (recipes[row][col] == NOTHING) {\n                break;\n            }\n            printf(\" %s\", recipes[row][col]);\n        }\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n    }\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_multi.php"},
			{"title": "Your Level 4 challenges",
			"text": "Three challenges (coins [b]and EXP[/b]). Goals: 8 of each new dish and 1600 profit:\n• [b]Recipe in an array[/b]: fill an array with [b]recipe()[/b] and loop over it ([code]items[i][/code]) to make and serve a dish.\n• [b]One loop, any dish[/b]: serve [b]2 different dishes[/b] in one Run with the same recipe() loop.\n• [b]Measure with sizeof[/b]: use [b]sizeof[/b] to work out an array's length in a Run that serves a dish.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int items[8];\n    int room = sizeof(items) / sizeof(items[0]);   // 8\n    printf(\"Room for %d ingredients\\n\", room);\n    // while (1) { ...take_order(), recipe(order, items), loop, serve()... }\n}", "w3": "c_arrays_size.php"},
		],
		"practice": {"task": "Make one program that can cook ANY order: in a loop, take take_order(), fill an array with recipe(), loop over the items, cook each one at cook_station() if needed, plate it, then assemble and serve.",
			"code": "void main() {\n    int items[8];\n    while (1) {\n        if (has_order()) {\n            int order = take_order();\n            int n = recipe(order, items);\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n                // fridge -> pick_up(items[i]) -> cook if needed -> plate\n            }\n            assemble(order);\n            serve();\n        } else {\n            wait(1);\n        }\n    }\n}"},
	},
	{
		"level": 5, "title": "Functions", "concept": "Writing and reusing your own functions",
		"pages": [
			{"title": "Why write functions?",
			"text": "Since Lesson 1 you've been [b]calling[/b] functions: printf(), go_to(), cook(), recipe(). Now you'll [b]write your own[/b].\n\nA [b]function[/b] is a named block of code that runs only when it is called. You write the steps once and use them as often as you like, which keeps programs short and easy to fix.",
			"code": "", "w3": "c_functions.php"},
			{"title": "Create and call",
			"text": "• Create a function with a [b]return type[/b], a [b]name[/b], ( ) and { }.\n• [b]void[/b] means it gives nothing back.\n• Write it [b]above[/b] main(), then [b]call[/b] it with its name, ( ) and ;\n• Call it as many times as you like.",
			"code": "void wash_up() {\n    go_to(SINK);\n    printf(\"Clean!\\n\");\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    wash_up();\n    wash_up();\n}", "w3": "c_functions.php"},
			{"title": "Declaring before defining",
			"text": "C reads your program from top to bottom, so a function must be known [b]before[/b] it is called.\n\nEither write the whole function above main(), or put a [b]declaration[/b] (just the first line, ending in ;) above main() and the full [b]definition[/b] below it.",
			"code": "int double_price(int price);   // declaration\n\nvoid main() {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", double_price(30));\n}\n\nint double_price(int price) {  // definition\n    return price * 2;\n}", "w3": "c_functions_decl.php"},
			{"title": "Parameters and arguments",
			"text": "• A [b]parameter[/b] is a variable in the function's ( ) that receives a value.\n• The value you pass when calling is the [b]argument[/b], just like FRIDGE in go_to(FRIDGE).\n• Several parameters are separated by commas, each with its type.\n• The arguments must match the parameters in [b]number and order[/b].",
			"code": "void cook_at(int item, int station) {\n    go_to(FRIDGE);\n    pick_up(item);\n    go_to(station);\n    cook();\n    go_to(ASSEMBLY);\n    plate();\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    cook_at(RICE, GRILL);   // item = RICE, station = GRILL\n    cook_at(EGG, STOVE);\n    assemble(EGSILOG);\n    serve();\n}", "w3": "c_functions_parameters.php"},
			{"title": "Return values",
			"text": "To send a value back, give the function a type such as [b]int[/b] instead of void, and use [b]return[/b].\n\n• return ends the function immediately and hands the value to the caller.\n• Store the result in a variable, or use it straight away in printf or an if.",
			"code": "int price_for(int count) {\n    return count * 30;\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    int total = price_for(3);\n    printf(\"%d coins\\n\", total);\n    if (price_for(5) > 100) {\n        printf(\"Big order!\\n\");\n    }\n}", "w3": "c_functions_parameters.php"},
			{"title": "Arrays as parameters",
			"text": "Pass a whole array by writing its name without [ ]. In the parameter list, write [b]type name[][/b], and pass the length too.\n\nChanges the function makes to the array's items are visible to the caller: the array is shared, not copied.",
			"code": "void make(int items[], int n, int dish) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        go_to(FRIDGE);\n        pick_up(items[i]);\n        int where = cook_station(items[i]);\n        if (where != NOTHING) {\n            go_to(where);\n            cook();\n        }\n        go_to(ASSEMBLY);\n        plate();\n    }\n    assemble(dish);\n    serve();\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    int egsilog[] = {RICE, EGG};\n    make(egsilog, 2, EGSILOG);\n}", "w3": "c_functions_parameters.php"},
			{"title": "Scope: where variables live",
			"text": "• A variable created [b]inside[/b] a function is [b]local[/b]: only that function can use it, and it disappears when the function ends.\n• A variable created [b]outside[/b] all functions is [b]global[/b]: every function can use and change it.\n• Avoid using the same name for a global and a local variable: it's confusing.",
			"code": "int served = 0;            // global: shared by every function\n\nvoid count_one() {\n    int bonus = 5;         // local: only count_one() knows it\n    served++;\n    printf(\"Bonus %d\\n\", bonus);\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    count_one();\n    count_one();\n    printf(\"Served %d\\n\", served);\n}", "w3": "c_scope.php"},
			{"title": "Your Library",
			"text": "Functions you'll use again belong in your [b]Library (L)[/b]: every switched-on Library slot can be called from every staff member's program. While programming a staff member, the [b]My Library[/b] panel inserts a call in one click.\n\nNow you know everything the kitchen teaches: variables, arithmetic, decisions, loops, arrays and functions. Combine them into one great restaurant program!",
			"code": "void main() {\n    while (1) {\n        if (has_order()) {\n            int order = take_order();\n            printf(\"Order: %s\\n\", order);\n        } else {\n            wait(1);\n        }\n    }\n}", "w3": "c_functions_decl.php"},
			{"title": "Your Level 5 challenges",
			"text": "Three challenges (coins [b]and EXP[/b]), each in a Run that serves food. Goals: 6 of each new dish and 2500 profit:\n• [b]Your own function[/b]: a function with a [b]parameter[/b], called at least twice.\n• [b]Return a value[/b]: call a function that [b]returns[/b] something (int, float...).\n• [b]Use your Library[/b]: call a function saved in a [b]Library[/b] slot (L).\nPut make_dish() in your Library, then serve with it and print a price from a returning function.",
			"code": "int price_of(int dish) {\n    if (dish == EGSILOG) {\n        return 30;\n    }\n    return 50;\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    int order = take_order();\n    make_dish(order);              // from your Library\n    printf(\"About %d coins\\n\", price_of(order));\n}", "w3": "c_functions_return.php"},
		],
		"practice": {"task": "Write make_dish(int dish) that uses recipe() and cook_station() to cook and serve any dish. Then call it from a main() loop that serves every order. Save make_dish in your Library so every staff member can use it.",
			"code": "void make_dish(int dish) {\n    int items[8];\n    int n = recipe(dish, items);\n    // loop over items: fetch, cook if needed, plate\n    assemble(dish);\n    serve();\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    while (1) {\n        if (has_order()) {\n            make_dish(take_order());\n        } else {\n            wait(1);\n        }\n    }\n}"},
	},
